WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.576
A trader buys goods ready to sell, so its cost of sales uses purchases. A manufacturer makes its own goods, so it uses production cost from the manufacturing account instead.

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:22.268
Only finished goods inventories go in the income statement. Raw materials and work in progress stay in the manufacturing account.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:35.736
Shared costs, such as rent, are split. Say rent is ten thousand, eighty per cent factory. Eight thousand goes to the manufacturing account, and the office share, two thousand, goes to the income statement.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:51.593
Write the heading and the revenue. Then build cost of sales: opening finished goods, plus production cost, plus any finished goods bought in, less closing finished goods. Revenue minus cost of sales is gross profit.

00:00:52.150 --> 00:01:04.111
Next, the expenses. Adjust each shared expense for any amount owing or prepaid first. Then take only the office share. Do the same for depreciation.

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:11.747
Finally, total the administrative and selling expenses and subtract them from gross profit to reach profit for the year.

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:26.272
Here is a real question from June 2025, Paper 2. Prepare Josh's income statement for the year ended thirty-first March 2025. Thirteen marks.

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:29.392
Here is the table of Josh's balances, part one.

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:30.889
And part two.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:42.100
From the data, use only the finished goods inventories: twenty thousand five hundred at the start and thirty-one thousand one hundred and ten at the end. Josh also bought finished goods for fourteen thousand.

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:54.837
Cost of sales: twenty thousand five hundred, plus production cost of three hundred and forty-nine thousand eight hundred and eighty-two, plus fourteen thousand, less thirty-one thousand one hundred and ten. That is three hundred and fifty-three thousand two hundred and seventy-two.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:02:03.478
Revenue is five hundred and two thousand five hundred. Take away cost of sales: gross profit is one hundred and forty-nine thousand two hundred and twenty-eight.

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:20.608
Now the shared costs, twenty per cent to the office. Electricity has one thousand four hundred owing, so add it first. Rent has eight thousand nine hundred prepaid, so take it off first. Depreciation is fifteen per cent reducing balance on the carrying value.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:36.970
Add administrative expenses, carriage outwards and administrative wages. The expenses total sixty-nine thousand two hundred and seventy-eight, so profit for the year is seventy-nine thousand nine hundred and fifty. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:48.346
A common mistake is using raw materials or work in progress inventories in cost of sales. Only finished goods inventories belong here.

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:56.048
Another is taking twenty per cent of the rent before taking off the prepayment. Adjust first, then apportion.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:09.106
Now you try. Tomas makes bicycles. Prepare his income statement to profit for the year. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:21.141
Cost of sales is fifteen thousand, plus one hundred and twenty thousand, plus six thousand, less eleven thousand: one hundred and thirty thousand. Gross profit is seventy thousand.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:32.515
Rent for the year is twenty thousand, and the office share is five thousand. Add office wages: expenses are thirty-five thousand. Profit for the year is thirty-five thousand.

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:43.459
Remember: finished goods and production cost give cost of sales, then gross profit, then the office share of each adjusted expense. Now try the practice questions.
